1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,080
Bytecode runs inside a model of memory. If
you only remember "objects on the heap,"
you already have a useful half-truth.

2
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:20,480
The other half — the stack — explains
recursion limits, local variables, and why
a crash dump's stack trace is not a heap
dump. The stack is per-call bookkeeping.
The heap is where objects live
(generally).

3
00:00:20,599 --> 00:00:32,079
Mixing those stories is how people misread
incidents. Look at this java for a moment.
When f is invoked, the JVM creates a stack
frame for that call on the current
thread's stack.

4
00:00:32,199 --> 00:00:40,479
The frame holds locals — including x and
the reference u — plus bookkeeping for
return. new User() allocates an object on
the heap.

5
00:00:40,599 --> 00:00:49,439
The local variable does not contain the
whole object; it contains a reference
pointing at the heap object. When f
returns, the frame is discarded.

6
00:00:49,559 --> 00:01:03,719
If nothing else references that User, the
object becomes eligible for garbage
collection later. The reference died with
the frame; the object's fate depends on
reachability. Frames hold locals and
return info.

7
00:01:03,839 --> 00:01:12,399
Deep recursion creates many frames and can
throw StackOverflowError. Huge or many
objects can exhaust the heap and throw
OutOfMemoryError.

8
00:01:12,519 --> 00:01:17,079
Those errors sound similar in a chat
message and mean different missing
resources.

9
00:01:17,199 --> 00:01:29,759
Confusing heap dumps with stack traces is
a classic ops mix-up: one shows objects
and retention; the other shows who called
whom when something blew up. You often
need both, but they answer different
questions.

10
00:01:29,879 --> 00:01:43,560
Escape analysis may elide allocations. The
JIT sometimes proves an object never
leaves a method and can allocate it in a
way that avoids a full heap object — or
scale it into registers and stack-like
storage.

11
00:01:43,679 --> 00:01:54,759
That is why "every new always hits the
heap forever" is too strong. You need
enough humility to know optimizations can
change allocation stories after warmup.
Look at this java for a moment.

12
00:01:54,879 --> 00:02:03,399
Whether boxed becomes a real heap object
in steady state depends on escape and JIT
decisions. Microbenchmarks that ignore
warmup lie about these effects.

13
00:02:03,519 --> 00:02:15,879
GC cares about heap reachability from GC
roots — including references in stack
frames, static fields, and JNI handles. An
object is collectible when no GC root can
reach it.

14
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:26,639
Stack frames are temporary roots for their
locals. That is why a long-lived thread
stuck in a method can pin objects through
locals, and why finishing the method can
suddenly make a large graph collectible.

15
00:02:26,759 --> 00:02:38,079
What if we ignore native and stack limits
while staring only at -Xmx? We miss
StackOverflowError, thread stack sizing,
and native memory. Heap is central, not
total.

16
00:02:38,199 --> 00:02:45,759
Thread stacks have size limits configured
by the runtime. Many threads mean many
stacks — a footprint cost separate from
the heap.

17
00:02:45,879 --> 00:03:00,319
Virtual threads change the economics of
stack usage for blocking workloads, but
the conceptual split remains: frame state
versus heap objects. When you read a stack
overflow, look at recursion depth and huge
frames.

18
00:03:00,439 --> 00:03:03,759
When you read a heap OOM, look at
retention charts.

19
00:03:03,879 --> 00:03:18,280
A heap dump answers "what objects dominate
and who retains them?" A stack trace
answers "what was this thread doing?" An
incident that shows both — a thread stuck
holding a large structure in locals while
others allocate

20
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:20,920
 — teaches why the two views belong
together.

21
00:03:21,039 --> 00:03:35,280
Picture a recursive XML parser that dies
with StackOverflowError on a hostile deep
document. The heap may be fine. Raising
heap size does nothing. Bounding depth or
switching to an explicit stack structure
does.

22
00:03:35,399 --> 00:03:46,319
The stack/heap split decides the fix
family before you touch a single flag.
Picture the opposite: a cache growing
without eviction until OutOfMemoryError:
Java heap space.

23
00:03:46,439 --> 00:03:52,839
Stack traces show random request threads;
the heap dump shows the cache. Different
tool, different truth.

24
00:03:52,959 --> 00:04:04,879
Hold a practical checklist: frames for
locals and control; heap for objects; SO
vs OOM as different resources; heap dumps
vs stack traces as different questions;

25
00:04:04,999 --> 00:04:08,879
escape analysis as a reason to measure
before declaring allocation fate.

26
00:04:08,999 --> 00:04:17,719
Prefer the right dump for the question. If
the question is retention, dump the heap.
If the question is "who called what," read
the stack.

27
00:04:17,839 --> 00:04:26,879
The next question is the reclaim story
itself: how and when unused objects go
away, and what your allocation rate does
to that pace.
